=== agent-deck connect: ChatGPT (via Codex CLI or the Responses API) (name "chatgpt", scopes read) ===
Token file: /home/me/.local/share/agent-deck/assistants/chatgpt.token (0600). Revoke: agent-deck connect revoke chatgpt
ChatGPT web and desktop apps cannot send a fixed token yet (they only do OAuth), so use Codex CLI (same ChatGPT login) or the Responses API. The Responses API calls the URL from its own cloud, so localhost does not work. Only with the owner's consent: run `agent-deck web --no-tui --token-file OWNER_TOKEN_FILE --allowed-host HOST` (the owner token keeps the owner pages locked), expose it over HTTPS (for example `tailscale funnel 8420`) and re-run this command with --url https://HOST.

--- 1. Config: Codex CLI: run the command. Responses API: the "tools" array of your request ---
# Codex CLI (signs in with your ChatGPT account):
codex mcp add agentdeck -- /usr/local/bin/agent-deck connect serve --token-file /home/me/.local/share/agent-deck/assistants/chatgpt.token

# OpenAI Responses API: add to "tools" (send it with every request; the token is not stored):
{
  "type": "mcp",
  "server_label": "agentdeck",
  "server_description": "agent-deck: terminal session manager for AI coding agents",
  "server_url": "http://127.0.0.1:8420/mcp",
  "authorization": "adk_GOLDENTOKEN",
  "allowed_tools": [
    "whoami",
    "list_sessions",
    "session_status",
    "read_output"
  ],
  "require_approval": {
    "never": {
      "tool_names": [
        "whoami",
        "list_sessions",
        "session_status",
        "read_output"
      ]
    }
  }
}

--- 2. Test ---
agent-deck connect test --name chatgpt

--- 3. Guide: keep this text in Codex: ~/.agents/skills/agent-deck/SKILL.md. Responses API: the "instructions" of your request ---
You are connected to agent-deck on my machine as "chatgpt" with scopes: read.
agent-deck is a terminal session manager for AI coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, OpenCode and others): each session is one agent working in its own terminal. Through the agent-deck tools you can see those sessions and, when allowed, drive them.

Tools (MCP server "agentdeck"; the HTTP API at http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1 mirrors them):
- whoami: your name, scopes and limits
- list_sessions {group?, status?}: all sessions with id, title, tool, status, group, path (read)
- session_status {session}: one session's details (read)
- read_output {session}: the last answer of that session's agent (read)
- send_message {session, message}: type a message into a session (send)
- approve {session, choice?}: answer a visible approval prompt (approve)
- launch_session {path, title, tool?, message?}: start a new session (launch)

Rules:
1. Call list_sessions first and refer to sessions by id.
2. Read before acting: check session_status or read_output before you send or approve anything.
3. ASK THE USER and wait for a clear yes before send_message, approve or launch_session; show the exact text you will send.
4. Never stop, delete, restart or kill sessions. There is no tool for it; do not use a shell or any other way to do it.
5. Never read, print or change credentials, tokens or agent-deck's own files (~/.agent-deck, ~/.local/share/agent-deck).
6. If a tool answers "scope missing", do not work around it: tell the user the exact command from the answer (for example: agent-deck connect chatgpt --scopes read,send).
7. Keep reports short: one line per session (title, status, what it is waiting for).

To allow more, run: agent-deck connect chatgpt --scopes read,send,approve,launch (pick only the scopes you need; this rotates the token, stdio configs pick the new one up from the token file, HTTP configs need the new token)
=== end ===
